# Opening a workbench, as an ordinary program.
#
# This was `workbench.open_workbench`, and it was on a list of natives to add — which was the mistake.
# A native is for a primitive the kernel cannot express; this is three loops and a copy. What actually
# blocked it was that the instruction set could not ask a node what shape it was, and once `NLABELS` /
# `LABEL_AT` / `NKEYS` / `KEY_AT` / `KIND` exist it needs nothing else. Four of the six "natives" first
# proposed for the goal machinery turned out to be plain edge reads, and this was one of the two that
# were composites.
#
# The original -> image map is `SETREF` / `DEREF` on the scratch node, keyed by node id. A stored
# reference takes a string key and a node id is a string, so this is an O(1) map built out of opcodes
# that were already there — no new mechanism, and `DEREF` answering `None` for an absent key is exactly
# the "was this node copied?" test the second pass needs.

# Copy everything reachable from `subject` into a fresh workbench, and mint frame 0 with one mapping per
# copied node.
#
# Three passes, in the order the Python did them, because the order decides the node ids and a copy that
# allocated them differently would be a different graph for no reason: mint every image, then relink the
# edges among them, then mint the mappings. The second pass cannot run early — a copy cannot link to an
# image that does not exist yet.
#
# Edge properties ARE carried now — by `copy_set`, which owns the copying half of this and is shared with
# `workbench.step`. This used to inline both passes and to drop edge properties on the way, which was a
# real gap rather than a simplification; `reachable.mf` records what closing it took.
#
# `parent` nests this workbench inside another — subgoal exploration, where a mapping's `original` points
# one level up rather than at the real graph, so resolving to a real node is a walk. `explores` attaches
# the hypothesis whose assumptions this workbench is investigating: hypotheses are *run* on workbenches
# rather than being a second mechanism. Both may be nothing.
#
# **Every mapping is indexed as it is minted**, and that is not bookkeeping: the frame carries a stored
# reference from identity to the mapping holding this frame's version, and resolution reads it with one
# `DEREF`. Locating a version by walking the reverse index instead is O(versions) with an allocation per
# hop — measured at 30x on the search-heavy checks. Written by whoever mints the mapping, because that is
# the only place that knows: here, in `step`, and in the writer. Deleting the `SETREF` below does not
# degrade anything gently; the next step raises on an unset register.
fn open_workbench(subject, label, parent, explores) -> thing:
    NEW R(wb) "workbench"
    SET R(wb) "label" F(label)
    CONST R(depth) 0
    JMPNOT F(parent) .no_parent
    ATTR R(pd) F(parent) "depth"
    JMPIF R(pd) .have_depth
    CONST R(pd) 0
.have_depth:
    ADD R(depth) R(pd) 1
    LINK R(wb) "parent" F(parent)
.no_parent:
    SET R(wb) "depth" R(depth)
    JMPNOT F(explores) .no_hypothesis
    LINK R(wb) "explores" F(explores)
.no_hypothesis:
    LINK R(wb) "subject" F(subject)
    INVOKE R(walk) reachable start=F(subject)
    INVOKE R(walk) copy_set walk=R(walk)
    COUNT R(n) R(walk) "found"

.frame:
    NEW R(frame) "frame"
    SET R(frame) "index" 0
    LINK R(wb) "root_frame" R(frame)
    LINK R(wb) "frame" R(frame)
    CONST R(i) 0
.map:
    LT R(more_m) R(i) R(n)
    JMPNOT R(more_m) .done
    GET_AT R(o) R(walk) "found" R(i)
    DEREF R(img) R(walk) R(o)
    NEW R(m) "mapping"
    LINK R(m) "original" R(o)
    LINK R(m) "image" R(img)
    LINK R(frame) "mapping" R(m)
    SETREF R(frame) R(o) R(m)
    ADD R(i) R(i) 1
    JMP .map

.done:
    DROP R(walk)
    COPY R(result) R(wb)
